Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

§27.2.1.3.1/.2 make a promise resolve/reject function an anonymous BUILT-IN
function object. Standalone materialized them as a bare `$__promise_settle_cap`
closure struct: callable, with a `length` inherited from the `$arity` header,
but with no `name`, `null` for `[[Prototype]]`, only one own property name, and
no [[Construct]] refusal — so every reflective row over the escaped closure
failed.

Mechanism — reuse the carrier, do not add a second one. `$__promise_settle_cap`
now SUBTYPES the repository's builtin-function metadata type
(`ensureBuiltinFnMetaType`, `{name: "", length: 1}`) instead of the bare
signature wrapper, so the finalize-time arms that already answer `name`/`length`
/gOPD/delete/getOwnPropertyNames (`fillBuiltinFnMeta`) and
isExtensible/isFrozen/isSealed/getPrototypeOf (`prependBuiltinFnObjectSemantics`)
cover it for free. Both settle functions share ONE metadata entry because the
spec gives them identical `{name, length}`. The capture moves from field 3 to
field 5; `capPromiseFieldIdx` carries that index and one factory
(`buildPromiseSettleClosureInstrs`) owns the `struct.new` operand order for all
three mint sites (executor, executor-value, thenable job), so they cannot drift.

§7.2.4 IsConstructor at a dynamic `new` site: a new `__builtinfn_is_builtin`
native — filled from the SAME finalized predicate as the integrity helpers, so
the answers cannot disagree — lets the two standalone unknown-ctor bases throw
the spec TypeError for a built-in function value. Deliberately narrow: it fires
only for a registered metadata family, never for a user `function f(){}`, and
emits nothing when the object runtime is absent. Placed after
ArgumentListEvaluation, per §13.3.5.1 step 3.

Measured, `built-ins/Promise/**` ES2015 corpus, `--standalone`, 140 rows:
baseline 0 pass / 134 fail / 6 compile_error. Slice B flips six rows to pass —
`{resolve,reject}-function-{name,property-order,prototype}.js` — with zero
possible regression inside the corpus (nothing passed before) and all six
verified host-import clean (#5272: the in-process probe does not apply that
check, so it was run separately).

Not fixed here, and why: the six `executor-function-*` rows exercise
GetCapabilitiesExecutor via `Promise.resolve.call(NotPromise)` and need Slice D's
generic NewPromiseCapability; `exec-args.js` and the two `*-nonconstructor.js`
rows fail earlier, in the harness-level `var` binding (the receiver reads null
before any of this is consulted) — the same error text as at baseline.

